            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "Who is asking for this change? Is there a production case ?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Tan Siewert",
                        "email": "tan@siewert.io",
                        "username": "sinuscosinustan"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, the HPE ProLiant Gen11 platform has one 64M (512Mb) SPI flash that contains both UEFI partitions. Still under discussion with HPE how they switch between those partitions for the host, but from what we've been already told they copy the data between A and B in memory somehow via a `vrom`-based setup.\n\nSee https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-bmc-code-mgmt/+/91736/comment/8a6ef119_4a17b58e/ for more information."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> Who is asking for this change? \n\nI am asking for this change because it is a feature of the HPE machines we want to support.\n\n> Is there a production case ?\n\nConceptually it makes sense to be able to have A/B for the host SPI to provide a fallback host fw and in case of interrupted update / power outage. So yes, there is a production case."
                }
            ],
